# Line endings — git decides, not whichever tool last wrote the file.
#
# Every text file is stored with LF (what the repo already holds) and checked out with the platform's
# own endings. Without a rule here the stored form is whatever the writer produced, and an editor that
# rewrote one file the other way turned a 20-line change into a 4128-line diff (2026-09-17) that had to
# be rebuilt by hand before it could be reviewed. This commit changes no file content: with everything
# already LF, `git add --renormalize .` staged nothing but this file.
* text=auto

# Run by a POSIX shell or by NSIS/Windows — these keep the endings their interpreter expects,
# whatever the platform.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.nsh text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary: never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.PNG binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.svg text
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.ttf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
*.xlsx binary
*.sqlite binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.hdr binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.node binary
*.zip binary
*.blockmap binary
*.dmg binary
